cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "PhantomLoad: measuring the gap between enrolled and delivered demand response capacity"
message: "If you use this repository or its findings, please cite it."
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Morrissey
    given-names: Andrew
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0004-8758-2687"
    affiliation: "independent"
version: 0.4.3
date-released: "2026-10-07"
license: Apache-2.0
keywords:
  - demand response
  - grid reliability
  - utility economics
  - capacity market
  - EIA-861
abstract: >-
  US utilities realise about 39 percent of the demand response peak reduction
  they report as available, a gap that has held for twelve years. Using
  EIA-861 utility-level files for 2016 to 2018, which reconcile to the national
  series to the megawatt, this work decomposes that gap into three components
  that behave differently. Capacity reported at exactly zero reads as
  non-dispatch rather than failed dispatch, but only 15 percent of it belongs
  to utilities with a measured delivery in another year, so whether it was
  available is mostly undetermined. Its apparent concentration outside
  organised markets, and that of capacity with no reported outcome, rests
  mostly on four Florida utilities. Whether partial delivery, about half the
  gap, responds to system conditions is specification-dependent. Nearly a
  third of positive reports set actual exactly equal to potential, a
  convention that produced an apparent size gradient this work first reported
  and then withdrew. Priced against PJM's published cost of new entry, demand
  response is cheaper in six of eight defensible pairings of accounting bases.
  The public record does not support reading the gap as waste, nor does it
  establish the opposite, and the work states what data would settle it.
